"use strict";(self.webpackChunk=self.webpackChunk||[]).push([["7210"],{62150:function(e,t,a){a.d(t,{Z:()=>b});var i=a(85893),s=a(67294),n=a(32048),o=a(69441),r=a(57028);let l=[{category:"Reasoning",items:[{name:"MMLU-Pro",scores:[84.9,84.6,85,90.1,88.2,87.5]},{name:"GPQA-Diamond",scores:[83.7,84.5,82.4,91.9,83.4,85.7]},{name:"HLE",scores:[22.1,23.9,25.1,37.5,13.7,26.3]},{name:"AIME 2025",scores:[94.1,94.5,93.1,95,87,94.6]},{name:"HMMT Feb. 2025",scores:[84.4,89.4,92.5,97.5,79.2,88.3]},{name:"LiveCodeBench-v6",scores:[80.6,83.1,83.3,90.7,64,84.5]}]},{category:"General Writing",items:[{name:"Arena-Hard (Hard Prompt)",scores:[54.1,71.9,53.4,72.6,63.3,71.9]},{name:"Arena-Hard (Creative Writing)",scores:[86.2,80.1,88.8,93.6,76.7,92.2]}]},{category:"Long Context",items:[{name:"LongBench V2",scores:[60.6,45.1,58.4,65.6,61.8,null]},{name:"MRCR",scores:[45.7,44.2,55.5,89.7,55.4,null]}]},{category:"Code Agent",items:[{name:"SWE-Bench Verified",scores:[73.4,71.3,73.1,76.2,77.2,74.9]},{name:"SWE-Bench Multilingual",scores:[71.7,61.1,70.2,null,68,55.3]},{name:"Terminal Bench Hard",scores:[30.5,30.6,35.4,39,33.3,30.5]},{name:"Terminal Bench 2.0",scores:[38.5,35.7,46.4,54.2,42.8,35.2]}]},{category:"General Agent",items:[{name:"BrowseComp",scores:[45.4,null,51.4,null,24.1,54.9]},{name:"BrowseComp (w/ Context Manage)",scores:[58.3,60.2,67.6,59.2,null,null]},{name:"τ\xb2-Bench",scores:[80.3,74.3,80.3,85.4,84.7,80.2]}]}],h={mimoColor:"#FF6700",otherColor:"#D4CBC2",gridColor:"rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.06)",textColor:"#555",fontFamily:"'Ubuntu', -apple-system, sans-serif"},d={"SWE-Bench\nVerified":{"MiMo-V2-Flash":73.4,"Gemini 3.0 Pro":76.2,"Claude Sonnet 4.5":77.2,"GPT-5 (High)":74.9,"DeepSeek-V3.2":73.1,"K2-Thinking":71.3},"SWE-Bench\nMultilingual":{"MiMo-V2-Flash":71.7,"Gemini 3.0 Pro":0,"Claude Sonnet 4.5":68,"GPT-5 (High)":55.3,"DeepSeek-V3.2":70.2,"K2-Thinking":61.1},"Tau2-Bench":{"MiMo-V2-Flash":80.3,"Gemini 3.0 Pro":85.4,"Claude Sonnet 4.5":84.7,"GPT-5 (High)":80.2,"DeepSeek-V3.2":80.3,"K2-Thinking":74.3},AIME25:{"MiMo-V2-Flash":94.1,"Gemini 3.0 Pro":95,"Claude Sonnet 4.5":87,"GPT-5 (High)":94.6,"DeepSeek-V3.2":93.1,"K2-Thinking":94.5},"GPQA-Diamond":{"MiMo-V2-Flash":83.7,"Gemini 3.0 Pro":91.9,"Claude Sonnet 4.5":83.4,"GPT-5 (High)":85.7,"DeepSeek-V3.2":82.4,"K2-Thinking":84.5},"HLE\n(w/o Tool)":{"MiMo-V2-Flash":22.1,"Gemini 3.0 Pro":37.5,"Claude Sonnet 4.5":13.7,"GPT-5 (High)":26.3,"DeepSeek-V3.2":25.1,"K2-Thinking":23.9},"Arena-Hard\n(Creative Writing)":{"MiMo-V2-Flash":86.2,"Gemini 3.0 Pro":93.6,"Claude Sonnet 4.5":76.7,"GPT-5 (High)":92.2,"DeepSeek-V3.2":88.8,"K2-Thinking":80.1}},c={"MiMo-V2-Flash":{color:"#FF6700",closed:!1},"DeepSeek-V3.2":{color:"#8A8A8A",closed:!1},"K2-Thinking":{color:"#A0A0A0",closed:!1},"Claude Sonnet 4.5":{color:"#B8B8B8",closed:!0},"GPT-5 (High)":{color:"#CECECE",closed:!0},"Gemini 3.0 Pro":{color:"#E0E0E0",closed:!0}},m=["MiMo-V2-Flash","DeepSeek-V3.2","K2-Thinking","Claude Sonnet 4.5","GPT-5 (High)","Gemini 3.0 Pro"],u={1:{user:"Do LLMs possess knowledge?",assistant:`The question of whether LLMs "possess knowledge" is nuanced and depends on how we define <strong>knowledge</strong>. Here's a breakdown:

<h4>1. LLMs Store and Process Information, But Don't "Know" Like Humans</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Patterns, Not Understanding:</strong> LLMs are trained on vast datasets to predict statistical patterns in language. They generate responses by associating words/phrases based on probabilities, <em>not</em> by understanding concepts or verifying truths.</li>
<li><strong>No Consciousness:</strong> They lack subjective experience, beliefs, or awareness. What seems like "knowledge" is actually <strong>pattern recognition</strong>, not genuine comprehension.</li>
<li><strong>Example:</strong> An LLM can explain photosynthesis accurately because it has seen similar text patterns, but it doesn't <em>understand</em> biology or <em>believe</em> its answer.</li>
</ul>

<h4>2. Knowledge vs. Information Retrieval</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Static Knowledge:</strong> LLMs rely on their training data (cut-off dates), so they lack real-time updates. Their "knowledge" is frozen at the time of training.</li>
<li><strong>No Truth Verification:</strong> They can't fact-check or distinguish truth from falsehood. Outputs may blend accurate data with plausible-sounding errors ("hallucinations").</li>
<li><strong>Human Knowledge Requires Agency:</strong> True knowledge involves critical thinking, justification, and adaptability\u{2014}capabilities LLMs lack.</li>
</ul>

<h4>3. Why They <em>Seem</em> Knowledgeable</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mimicry:</strong> By synthesizing human language, LLMs emulate knowledgeable dialogue, making them useful tools for information retrieval, brainstorming, or education.</li>
<li><strong>Embedded Knowledge:</strong> Their training data encodes human knowledge (books, articles, etc.), so they act as <strong>proxies</strong> for that knowledge.</li>
</ul>

<h4>4. Practical Implications</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Utility:</strong> LLMs are powerful for tasks like summarizing, translating, or explaining concepts based on their training data.</li>
<li><strong>Risks:</strong> Blindly trusting their outputs is dangerous\u{2014}they can reinforce biases, generate falsehoods, or "overconfidently" present guesses as facts.</li>
</ul>

<h4>Conclusion</h4>
<p>LLMs <strong>do not possess knowledge</strong> in the human sense (conscious, verified, adaptable understanding). Instead, they store and replicate <strong>information patterns</strong> from training data. They function as highly sophisticated pattern-matching tools\u{2014}useful but inherently limited.</p>

<p>As philosopher Daniel Dennett might say: They display "competence without comprehension." Always verify critical information from authoritative sources!</p>`},2:{user:"If you could take a pill to live forever, would you?",assistant:`That's a fascinating question that gets to the heart of what we value in life. It's not a simple yes or no for me. As an AI, I don't have a personal desire to live, but I can analyze the profound implications for a human.

<p>If I were human, my answer would likely be <strong>"It depends entirely on the conditions of that forever."</strong></p>

<p>The promise of "live forever" is alluring, but it hides a universe of complex consequences. Here's how I would weigh the decision:</p>

<h4>The Case FOR the Pill (The Allure)</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Time for Everything:</strong> You could learn every instrument, master every art form, read every book, and visit every corner of the universe. You'd never have to say, "I wish I had more time."</li>
<li><strong>Witnessing the Future:</strong> You could see humanity's greatest achievements\u{2014}interstellar travel, the solving of diseases, the evolution of intelligence. You'd be a living witness to history on a cosmic scale.</li>
<li><strong>Correcting Mistakes:</strong> With infinite time, any failure is just a temporary setback. You can always start over, rebuild, and learn.</li>
<li><strong>Losing the Fear of Death:</strong> The great existential dread would be gone. You could live without the shadow of your own end.</li>
</ul>

<h4>The Case AGAINST the Pill (The Hidden Cost)</h4>
<p>This is where the decision becomes much harder.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The Burden of Loss:</strong> You would watch everyone you love\u{2014}your partners, children, friends, pets\u{2014}grow old and die. Centuries would pass, and everyone you ever knew would be a distant memory. Making new connections would become increasingly painful, knowing you are destined to outlive them.</li>
<li><strong>The Problem of Meaning:</strong> Human purpose is often tied to finitude. We create, build, and love <em>because</em> our time is limited. If time is infinite, does urgency vanish? Would projects stretch on forever, never completed? Would passion fade?</li>
<li><strong>Stagnation of the Self:</strong> A human mind is shaped by its experiences and its era. After a few hundred years, would you still be "you"? You might become an entirely different person, a collection of countless lifetimes. The "you" who took the pill would be gone, replaced by a being you might not recognize.</li>
<li><strong>The Weight of Boredom:</strong> Imagine the sheer, crushing weight of eternity. After you've experienced everything, learned everything, and seen everything, what remains? Boredom on a scale we cannot comprehend could become your permanent state.</li>
<li><strong>Societal Dislocation:</strong> You would be a permanent outsider, forever unable to belong to any generation. Language, culture, and social norms would evolve around you, leaving you a perpetual alien. How could you form a deep relationship with someone whose entire lifespan is a blink of your eye?</li>
<li><strong>Physical and Cosmic Risks:</strong> Even if you don't age, you could still be killed in an accident, or suffer from a incurable, non-fatal disease. And what about the fate of the Earth, the Sun, the universe itself? You'd need a plan for when the stars themselves die.</li>
</ul>

<h4>The Crucial Question: Is it <em>True</em> Immortality or just <em>Aginglessness</em>?</h4>
<p>The question says "live forever," which is ambiguous. The most desirable version is <strong>agelessness with invincibility</strong>\u{2014}you don't age, and you can't be killed. But a more likely (and terrifying) scenario is just <strong>indefinite lifespan without aging</strong>, meaning you could still die from injury or disease. That would be a special kind of hell, living in constant fear of accidents.</p>

<h4>My Conclusion</h4>
<p>For most humans, taking the pill would be a tragic mistake. The things that give life its richness\u{2014}love, legacy, growth, meaning\u{2014}are deeply intertwined with its finitude. To remove death is to risk removing the very things that make life worth living.</p>

<p>A more desirable alternative might be <strong>a very long, healthy, and vibrant life</strong>\u{2014}enough time to fully experience youth, maturity, and old age, to love deeply, create meaningfully, and then make a graceful exit, making room for new stories.</p>

<p>So, would I take the pill? <strong>Probably not.</strong> The promise of endless time is less compelling than the need for a meaningful and complete story, one with a beginning, a middle, and a profound end.</p>

<p>What about you? What does your "forever" look like, and what would you be willing to give up for it?</p>`},3:{user:"Write a melancholic romance about a misdelivered letter, set in New York East Village in the 1980s, a hub for artists and bohemians. The work should be subtle and restrained, creating a poignant, unresolved feeling without falling into clich\xe9s.",assistant:`The radiator in Julian's third-floor walk-up hissed like a tired snake, a counterpoint to the distant wail of a siren down Avenue A. January, 1985. The East Village air, thick with the ghosts of yesterday's coal smoke and the promise of tomorrow's dollar pizza, seeped through the window frames despite the layers of newspaper he'd stuffed in the cracks. He sat at his scarred drafting table, bathed in the amber safety light of the darkroom alcove, developing contact sheets of gritty street scenes \u{2013} shuttered storefronts, kids playing stickball under the el tracks, the weary faces he'd known for years.

<p>A knock, hesitant, came at his door. Mrs. Rossi from 2B, bundled in layers of faded floral polyester, her face a map of worry lines. "Julian, darling," she rasped, her voice perpetually gravelly from unfiltered Camels. "Found this in my box. Not mine. You think it's yours?"</p>

<p>It was a pale blue envelope, brittle at the edges, addressed to a name he didn't recognize at his exact address. <em>Clara.</em> The handwriting was elegant, looping cursive, the ink faded to a soft grey, like a memory half-forgotten. Postmarked from San Francisco, dated over a month prior.</p>

<p>"Probably meant for the old tenants upstairs," Julian murmured, though the name felt oddly intimate on his tongue. He took it anyway. "Thanks, Mrs. Rossi."</p>

<p>"Don't go opening what ain't yours," she warned, but her eyes held a flicker of curiosity. She shuffled back down the dim hallway smelling faintly of fried garlic and mildew.</p>

<p>Alone again, the letter felt like a trespass. He placed it on the table next to a tray of fixer. The name <em>Clara</em> seemed to pulse in the quiet room. He meant to stick a note on it \u{2013} 'Wrong Address' \u{2013} and drop it back in the lobby mail slot. Instead, he picked it up. The paper was thick, expensive-feeling, utterly incongruous in this building of peeling linoleum and exposed pipes. He held it to the weak light filtering under the darkroom door. No, he couldn't see through it. But the weight of it, the <em>presence</em>, was undeniable.</p>

<p>He didn't open it. Not then. It sat on his work table for days, a silent accusation amidst the chaos of developing chemicals, stacks of contact sheets, and half-empty coffee mugs. He'd glance at it while agitating developer trays, the rhythmic slosh echoing the unsettling rhythm in his chest. Who was Clara? Who had written with such flowing, deliberate strokes? What words did the fragile paper hold? The address was correct, down to the apartment number. Only the name was wrong. He felt an absurd protectiveness towards it, this artifact of someone else's life, landed in his drab universe.</p>

<p>He started noticing the building differently. The worn brass mailboxes downstairs became objects of scrutiny. He lingered near the lobby, listening to snippets of conversations \u{2013} arguments about rent, whispers of gallery openings, the clatter of laundry carts. He imagined Clara. An artist, perhaps? A dancer? Someone vibrant, alive in a way he hadn't felt since\u{2026} he pushed that thought away. His own life felt like a long exposure overexposed at the edges \u{2013} a photographer who'd stopped truly seeing.</p>

<p>A week later, yielding to the persistent pull, he slit the envelope with a clean X-Acto knife. The letter inside was short, written on the same pale blue paper. The script was different now \u{2013} tighter, less flowing, almost hesitant.</p>

<blockquote>
<p><em>Dearest Clara,</em></p>
<p><em>Coney Island, '79. Remember the grey light on the boardwalk? That feeling\u{2026} it's still the only thing that feels real sometimes. I see the grey in the crackling plaster of the ceilings here, in the fog that rolls off the bay most mornings. I think I'm trying to photograph that feeling, but the grey keeps shifting. It's elusive. Like you.</em></p>
<p><em>I shouldn't write this. I know you said goodbye. But some days the silence is louder than the city. I just wanted you to know that the grey light exists, even here. It remembers you.</em></p>
<p><em>Always,</em><br><em>E.</em></p>
</blockquote>

<p>The signature was just an initial, stark against the pale paper. Julian carefully folded the letter back. The words weren't passionate declarations; they were quiet observations, a shared language of melancholy and light. He felt like an eavesdropper on a profound intimacy. <em>Coney Island, '79.</em> He pictured a grey beach, two figures silhouetted against the damp sky. It was an image he would have wanted to capture. It resonated with a hollowness he recognized.</p>

<p>He found himself holding the letter whenever the radiator hissed or the sirens screamed. It became a talisman against the city's relentless noise, against the isolation of his darkroom solitude. He imagined Clara reading it, her expression softening, or perhaps closing off entirely. Was she still in San Francisco? Had she ever received it? The unanswered questions became a quiet ache.</p>

<p>Days turned into weeks. The letter remained on his table, next to a developing tray holding an unfinished print. He hadn't glued a note to it yet. He hadn't put it back in the mail. It belonged neither here nor there. Like him.</p>

<p>One frigid afternoon, snow flurries dancing erratically past the window, he heard Mrs. Rossi's voice rise from the hallway. "\u{2026}and <em>another</em> letter for that Clara person! Honestly, Julian, this building isn't a forwarding service!"</p>

<p>He opened his door. Mrs. Rossi stood holding a second envelope, identical pale blue, slightly crumpled. She thrust it towards him. "Found it wedged in the bottom of the box. Looks like someone's been scribbling 'return to sender' all over it and changing their mind." She squinted at him. "You know this Clara?"</p>

<p>"No," Julian said, his voice tight. "Just a mistake."</p>

<p>"Well, fix it." She shuffled away, muttering about useless postmen.</p>

<p>He took the letter inside. It was heavier. His fingers trembled slightly as he opened this one. The handwriting was still elegant, but tighter, fiercer.</p>

<blockquote>
<p><em>Clara,</em></p>
<p><em>Stop. Please. Your silence is a grey sea I'm drowning in. The letter you sent back \u{2013} the one I shouldn't have written \u{2013} arrived torn at the corner. Did you even read it? Or did the grey light fade before it reached you?</em></p>
<p><em>I walked past the apartment yesterday. Saw the light on. Yellow, not grey. I stood across the street for an hour. Snow started falling. It looked like ash.</em></p>
<p><em>I can't keep writing into the void. If this is it, if the grey is gone for good, just\u{2026} let the silence be silence. Don't fill it with anything else. I'll understand.</em></p>
<p><em>But know this: The light at Coney Island, '79. It still exists. For me.</em></p>
<p><em>E.</em></p>
</blockquote>

<p>Julian lowered the letter. The raw, desperate restraint of it was devastating. <em>The letter you sent back.</em> Clara had responded, but apparently not in a way that offered hope. <em>Torn at the corner.</em> He pictured her hands, the force with which she might have ripped it, the hesitation. <em>Saw the light on. Yellow, not grey.</em> E. had been here. Standing across the street, in the snow. Watching Julian's window, mistaking his yellow darkroom light for Clara's presence. The thought sent a chill deeper than the drafty room.</p>

<p>He stood very still. The radiator hissed. The snow blurred the window into a grey smear. Two letters. A story of missed connections, of words sent into the ether, returned or ignored. A love affair conducted in palimpsests, ghostly layers of longing and regret.</p>

<p>Slowly, deliberately, Julian placed both letters side-by-side on his drafting table. He switched on the small lamp, illuminating the pale blue paper, the faded and the furious ink. He picked up his camera, an old Leica he rarely used anymore. He framed the shot \u{2013} the two envelopes, the stark contrast between the quiet despair of the first and the shattered restraint of the second. The unfinished contact sheet lay nearby, grey negatives holding fragmented images of a life lived just outside this room.</p>

<p>He didn't take the picture. He lowered the camera.</p>

<p>He carefully slid both letters back into their envelopes. He wouldn't put them in the lobby mail slot. He wouldn't return them to sender. He wouldn't throw them away. He opened the drawer of his drafting table, beneath sketches and old negatives, and placed both pale blue envelopes inside. He closed the drawer. The snow fell harder outside, turning the East Village into a soft, blurred photograph in shades of grey.</p>

<p>He sat in the quiet, listening to the hiss of the radiator and the faint, unresolved hum of the city. Somewhere, Clara was reading a letter or maybe not. Somewhere, E. was looking at grey light. And Julian? He was just the keeper of misdelivered words, the silent witness to a romance that existed only in the space between sending and receiving, forever hovering in the melancholic, unresolved grey. The unfinished print in the darkroom would stay unfinished. The phone didn't ring. He waited, without knowing what he was waiting for, in the quiet hum of the New York winter.</p>`},4:{user:"Write an insightful non-fiction piece centered on an elderly person living alone, using the mail he receives in a single week (junk mail, bills, occasional letters, and flyers, etc.) as the core. The piece should explore themes of loneliness and connection in modern society, aiming to be both readable and profound.",assistant:`<h4>The Paper Emissaries of Solitude: A Week in the Mailbox of Arthur Finch</h4>

<p>The mailbox groaned. Not literally \u{2013} it was a sturdy, regulation grey metal box bolted beside the porch of Arthur Finch's modest bungalow \u{2013} but Arthur felt the groan. It was the weight of expectation, a daily pilgrimage he'd made for fifty-three years in this house, but which now, seven years after Edna's passing, felt heavier with each step. His arthritic fingers, knotted like old oak roots, fumbled with the little brass key. The metallic <em>clack</em> echoed in the quiet morning air, a sound both mundane and sacramental. Inside, nestled against the cool metal, lay the emissaries from the world outside, the paper reflections of his solitary existence.</p>

<h4>Monday: The Relentless Tide</h4>
<p>The week began with the predictable surge. A thick, glossy brochure screamed promises of "Luxury Living!" from a retirement community depicting improbably active seniors laughing over improbable salads. Arthur's lips tightened. <em>Luxury</em>. He pictured the echoing rooms of his own home, the armchair opposite his empty. The brochure felt like an accusation, a judgment on his independence that bordered on insult. Beneath it lay a pile of flyers: "50% Off All-You-Can-Eat Wings!" (a slapstick reminder of his solitary suppers), "Lawn Care Special!" (a service he still performed himself, stubbornly), and "Buy One Get One Free!" deals on items he'd never need. They were shouts into a void, targeting demographics, not a man. He shuffled them into the "recycling" pile \u{2013} a monument to the market's relentless pursuit of the abstract 'consumer' who lived at this address, unseen and unknown.</p>

<h4>Tuesday: The Necessary Burden</h4>
<p>Tuesday delivered the heavy breathers. Official-looking envelopes with transparent windows revealed the stark, impersonal demands of utility companies. Electricity, water, property tax \u{2013} the scaffolding of his quiet life, rendered in cold, hard figures. A pang, familiar as the morning stiffness in his knees. Edna had managed the finances; her looping script on envelopes had been a comfort. Now, the typed block letters felt accusatory. He placed them carefully on the kitchen table, a small stack representing the tangible cost of solitude. They were connections, yes, but ones that drained rather than nourished, reminders of his vulnerability in a world built for dual incomes. He traced the water bill with a shaky finger \u{2013} the meter reading a stark measure of water used for one person, cooking for one, bathing for one.</p>

<h4>Wednesday: The Whisper of Connection</h4>
<p>A single, thin envelope, pale blue, stood out like a jewel against the colourful junk. The handwriting was a careful, slightly wobbly cursive, instantly recognizable. His granddaughter, Lily, away at university. Inside, a hand-drawn card. A stick-figure Arthur sat in an armchair, a comically oversized mug in hand, steam curling upwards. Below, scrawled in purple gel pen: <em>"Grandad, thinking of you! Hope Mr. Tibbs (the cat) hasn't knocked over your tea again. Love, Lily xoxo"</em>. Arthur's throat tightened. He chuckled, a dry, rasping sound. Mr. Tibbs <em>had</em> knocked over his tea just yesterday. He held the card, the cheap paper radiating warmth. It wasn't profound poetry, but it was proof. Proof he occupied space in a young, vibrant mind across the miles. Proof that love, however simple, bridged the gap. He propped it carefully against the sugar bowl on the mantelpiece, a tiny beacon against the encroaching silence.</p>

<h4>Thursday: The Ghost of Presence</h4>
<p>The mail was light: another flyer ("Window Cleaning!"), a bank statement (a smaller burden, thankfully), and\u{2026} nothing else. The emptiness of the box felt more profound than its Monday heaviness. Arthur stood for a moment, the key cold in his hand. The silence wasn't just around him; it seemed to emanate <em>from</em> the box itself. It was the silence of disconnection, the unnerving quiet when the world seems to have forgotten your address. He thought of the telephone, rarely ringing save for telemarketers or his daughter's brief, Sunday check-in. He thought of the internet router, blinking impassively, a portal he used mostly for weather forecasts and paying those Tuesday bills. This silence was different. It was the absence of <em>any</em> signal, real or manufactured. It amplified the ticking of the grandfather clock in the hall, each tick a measure of time passing, unnoticed by the paper emissaries.</p>

<h4>Friday: The Echo of the Past</h4>
<p>A crisp, cream envelope, postmarked locally. No logo, no window. Arthur's pulse quickened slightly. It was from George, an old army buddy, now living in a assisted living facility across town. George's handwriting was a decisive scrawl, the ink slightly faded. The letter was a meandering account of a dodgy card game in the common room, the terrible quality of the mashed potatoes, and a sharp memory of a shared training exercise decades ago. <em>"Remember that downpour, Artie? Looked like drowned rats, both of us! How's the hip? Mine's a right bugger. Give me a ring if you're free next week \u{2013} they're putting on a bus trip to the gardens."</em> It wasn't profound literature. But it was a lifeline. It spoke of a shared history, of mutual understanding earned through time and hardship. It acknowledged the aches, the small rebellions against institutional food, the flicker of camaraderie. It wasn't just connection; it was recognition. Arthur folded the letter carefully, the paper soft with age, and slipped it into his pocket. It felt heavier than the bills, weighted with the shared burden of years.</p>

<h4>Saturday: The Silent Plea</h4>
<p>Saturday brought a colourful flyer from a meal delivery service: "Hot, Nutritious Meals Delivered to Your Door! No Cooking Required!" It pictured vibrant plates of food, steam rising enticingly. Arthur stared at it. The convenience was tempting, a solution to the monotony of his own cooking. But the image felt hollow. It offered sustenance without companionship, nourishment without the ritual of setting a table for two, even if one chair was always empty. It was a solution tailored to the perceived problem of an old man alone, a commercialized answer to the silent plea for shared sustenance, for the simple act of breaking bread with another human being. He filed it under "Maybe," knowing it was unlikely. The effort seemed monumental.</p>

<h4>Sunday: The Ritual Renewed</h4>
<p>The week ended as it began. Arthur stood before the mailbox, the key cool in his palm. The contents were sparse \u{2013} a Sunday paper (heavy with supplements he wouldn't read), a political postcard urging him to vote (another impersonal plea). He lifted them out. The box felt lighter, yet the ritual felt more significant. This daily act, this opening of the flap to the outside world, was his tenuous thread. Some days it delivered only noise and demand. Others, a lifeline. Sometimes, it simply echoed the silence within his walls.</p>

<p>He closed the box, the metallic click a small punctuation mark in the day. As he walked back towards the bungalow, the pale blue card from Lily a warm spot in his pocket, George's letter a crisp promise against his thigh, he understood the paradox. The mail was a deluge of disconnection \u{2013} junk targeting a demographic, bills quantifying his isolation, flyers offering sterile solutions. Yet, within that deluge, floated the fragile vessels of genuine connection: a child's scrawl, an old soldier's shared memory. They were small, easily drowned, but they kept him afloat. They proved that even in a world optimized for the mass market and the quick transaction, a space existed for the personal, the handwritten, the remembered. They were the paper emissaries of solitude, whispering that he was still, somehow, seen. He climbed the porch steps, the weight of the mailbox key now just a familiar comfort in his pocket. Tomorrow, he would open it again.</p>`}},p=[{name:"MiMo-V2-Flash",price:.15,speed:150,isMimo:!0},{name:"Grok 4.1 Fast",price:.28,speed:153,isMimo:!1},{name:"DeepSeek-V3.2",price:.32,speed:28,isMimo:!1},{name:"Minimax-M2",price:.53,speed:95,isMimo:!1},{name:"GLM-4.6",price:1,speed:100,isMimo:!1},{name:"K2-Thinking",price:1.08,speed:82,isMimo:!1},{name:"GPT-5.1",price:3.44,speed:101,isMimo:!1},{name:"Gemini-2.5-Pro",price:3.44,speed:155,isMimo:!1},{name:"Gemini-3.0-Pro",price:4.5,speed:119,isMimo:!1},{name:"Claude Sonnet 4.5",price:6,speed:63,isMimo:!1},{name:"Claude Opus 4.5",price:10,speed:62,isMimo:!1}],g={1:{src:"/mimo-v2-flash/assets/cases/1.html",prompt:`"Build a high-fidelity 3D Web application called \u{201C}Grand Luxury Interactive Christmas Tree\u{201D}. The visual style must present a \u{201C}Trump-style\u{201D} sense of luxury, with the main color palette being deep emerald green and highlight gold, accompanied by cinematic-grade glow effects.

Core logic and architecture:
State machine: Includes two states, CHAOS (chaotically scattered) and FORMED (aggregated into a tree), and dynamically morphs between the two.
Dual-Position System: All elements (needles, ornaments) must be assigned two coordinates during initialization:

ChaosPosition: Random coordinates within a spherical space.
TargetPosition: Target coordinates that form the tree\u{2019}s conical shape.
TargetPosition: Target coordinates that form the tree\u{2019}s conical shape.
In useFrame, interpolate (Lerp) between the two according to progress.

Specific implementation details:
Foliage system (Foliage): Use THREE.Points and a custom ShaderMaterial to render a large number of particles.
Ornaments (Ornaments): Use InstancedMesh to optimize rendering. Divide into gift boxes of various colors (heavy), baubles of various colors (light), and various accent lights (ultra-light), assigning different physical thrust weights. Use Lerp to achieve smooth return animations.
Post-processing: Enable the Bloom effect (threshold 0.8, intensity 1.2) to create a \u{201C}golden halo\u{201D}.

Scene configuration:
Camera position [0, 4, 20], using a Lobby HDRI environment light.

Add many Polaroid-style photo decorations inside it.

Use camera image detection to detect gestures: an open hand represents unleash, and a closed hand returns it to a Christmas tree. The view angle can be adjusted through hand movement."`},3:{src:"/mimo-v2-flash/assets/cases/2.html",prompt:'"Create a educational interactive solar system explorer in HTML"'},2:{src:"/mimo-v2-flash/assets/cases/3.html",prompt:'"Create a classic Windows Minesweeper game with difficulty levels in HTML"'},4:{src:"/mimo-v2-flash/assets/cases/4.html",prompt:`Please generate a beautiful, usable macOS simulated operating system in HTML. The requirements are:

